<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Afgooye</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Afgoi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Afgooye is a town on the Shabelle river in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, about 30 kilometres north-west of Mogadishu. From the early 18th century it was the seat of the Geledi clan and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobroon-dynasty">Gobroon dynasty</a>, who ruled the Sultanate of the Geledi in the fertile lower Shabelle valley. The town is known for its annual Istunka festival of mock stick fights.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afgooye.jpg" alt="Afgooye" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Guillain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Afgoye,_Somalia.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Afgooye lies about 30 kilometres from Mogadishu, in the Lower Shebelle region, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shebelle-river">Shabelle River</a> passes through the middle of the town. It is identified with the historical settlement of Lama Jiidle, &quot;two roads&quot; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a>, which Lee V. Cassanelli describes as the centre of Silcis administration. In Somali the name Afgooye means split mouth. It is one of the oldest towns of the lower Shebelle valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> the Silcis, a section of the Ajuran, governed the district from Lama Jiidle. They limited themselves to controlling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> and collecting taxes and tribute, and imposed a daily measure of grain on each household of the Geledi. Oral accounts say that when the sultan's daughter Imbia came to collect the tribute, the Geledi beat her instead. Her father, the sultan 'Umur Abukar Abroone, exclaimed &quot;waa la i afgooye&quot;, &quot;they have cut off my mouth&quot;, and the town took its name from the event. The Geledi, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-adeer">Ibrahim Adeer</a>, a general who had served the Ajuran, drove the Silcis out and established the Geledi Sultanate.</p><p>Under the Gobroon dynasty Afgooye reached its golden age as a trading hub between the coast and the interior, with caravans bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, leopard skins and aloe in exchange for cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, dates and firearms. In the early nineteenth century its population is estimated to have exceeded 80,000, and it was surrounded by a wall with five fortified gates. At the turn of the twentieth century Afgooye was incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Italian Somaliland</a>; the Italians occupied the town in 1908 and abolished its slave market, and a station on the Mogadishu–Villabruzzi Railway served it. After civil war broke out in 1991 parts of the town sheltered many internally displaced people, and on 25 May 2012 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> government forces backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union-mission-to-somalia">AMISOM</a> tanks recaptured it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-shabaab">Al-Shabaab</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town has about 79,400 inhabitants, and the wider Afgooye District is inhabited mainly by the Geledi and Garre clans, sub-clans of the Digil and Rahanweyn. It is the site of Lafoole College, the first college of education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, built on the site of the Battle of Lafoole of 1896. Afgooye is also known for the Istunka, an annual mock stick fight marking the New Year, which developed in the Ajuran period and was expanded by Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-yusuf-of-geledi">Ahmed Yusuf</a> into a tournament of teams with poets and dancers. The Afgooye datum, based on the Krasovsky ellipsoid, is a geodetic datum used in Somalia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Afgooye” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On the lower Shabelle, the Geledi clan around <b>Afgooye</b> rose to power and later founded the Sultanate of the Geledi, which dominated the river valley in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 18th century the Geledi clan, led by the Gobroon dynasty, rose to power at <b>Afgooye</b> on the Shabelle river after the fall of the Ajuran Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-the-geledi">Sultanate of the Geledi</a></li></ul></section>
